WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:03.970
Let's discuss the features and tools within Sequencer

2
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.610
and how they can impact your speed and efficiency when

3
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.160
using them.

4
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.039
Working our way numerically from one to five,

5
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:13.519
let's cover the Sequencer interface.

6
00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:15.560
In section one, this is the toolbar,

7
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.199
which is a collection of commonly used tools

8
00:00:18.199 --> 00:00:20.280
and settings within Sequencer.

9
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.600
We have the option to save the sequence,

10
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.440
browse to the sequence within the content browser,

11
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.920
create a new camera and set it as the current camera cut,

12
00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.799
export the sequence to a movie or image sequence

13
00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:36.869
via Movie Render Queue, open the Blueprint

14
00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:41.140
Director for the sequence, execute various actions.

15
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.140
Now, note, many of these actions

16
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.259
are available in the interface or with hotkeys.

17
00:00:47.259 --> 00:00:49.939
Change your interface layout and the information

18
00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:54.820
that's displayed, and modify your playback options.

19
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.200
Now, the next three menu options

20
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.920
are some of the most commonly used

21
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.259
when working with Sequencer.

22
00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:01.740
The first option gives the ability

23
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.859
to control default keying channels as well as

24
00:01:04.859 --> 00:01:07.150
keyframe interpolation.

25
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.349
The next option is Auto Keyframe,

26
00:01:09.349 --> 00:01:12.829
which will automatically create a key when parameters

27
00:01:12.829 --> 00:01:14.030
have changed.

28
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.549
A Sequencer will only create a key if a previous key has

29
00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:19.920
been created.

30
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.680
And lastly, we can restrict Sequencer's ability

31
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.200
to automatically create keys for properties

32
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.560
when they're modified within the sequence, the level,

33
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.250
or both.

34
00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.090
The Viewport Selectability button

35
00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.250
limits your ability to choose actors within the viewport.

36
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.569
When this button is active, you can only select objects

37
00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:40.659
using the sequence.

38
00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:43.159
You can disable snapping or enable it,

39
00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:46.200
as well as change the snapping interval.

40
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.939
You could modify the frame rate of the sequence.

41
00:01:48.939 --> 00:01:50.859
Finally, you can use the Curve Editor

42
00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:55.879
for granular control over your keyframes.

43
00:01:55.879 --> 00:01:57.680
Section 2 is the Outliner.

44
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.000
Actors and their properties that are part of the sequence

45
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.959
are displayed in this section.

46
00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:05.799
Note, typically only some of the actor's keyable properties

47
00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:09.360
will be shown here when the actor is added to the sequence.

48
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.240
You can add more keyable properties

49
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.000
using the Add button, the Plus button,

50
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.080
located on the Actors section, or by adding a keyframe

51
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.580
to the actor's properties from a detail panel.

52
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.460
Section 3 is our Timeline.

53
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.259
As the playhead advances, it will progress

54
00:02:27.259 --> 00:02:29.500
through the various keyframes, triggers,

55
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.610
and events based on their position on the timeline.

56
00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.629
Section 4 is the Selection tab, which

57
00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:40.250
can be docked or the default flyout,

58
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.370
and contains many of the same properties displayed

59
00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.159
when right-clicking an actor in the sequence.

60
00:02:46.159 --> 00:02:49.900
Lastly, Section 5 contains our playback controls,

61
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.060
buttons to specify our in and out

62
00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.300
points for the sequence or shot,

63
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.689
and our timeline zoom and view options.

64
00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:01.469
Now, Recent Updates to Sequencer

65
00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:03.569
allows you to filter tracks based

66
00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:05.729
on your Outliner selections.

67
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.889
You can isolate only the selected tracks,

68
00:03:07.889 --> 00:03:11.250
hide selected tracks, or display all.

69
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.530
After selecting a track or tracks,

70
00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.110
the Sequencer Curves Editor can also

71
00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:20.050
be used to manipulate keyframes and keyframe interpolations.

72
00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.330
Now, across the top of the Sequencer Curve Editor window

73
00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:26.870
are various features for displaying, manipulating,

74
00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.229
scaling, and snapping your keyframes.

75
00:03:30.229 --> 00:03:32.430
Additionally, you have the ability to set the keyframe

76
00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.469
interpolations, as well as the in and out timings

77
00:03:35.469 --> 00:03:38.360
for those keyframes.

78
00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.240
The Add Level Sequence with Shots tool

79
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.919
is useful for automatically generating a master

80
00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:46.719
sequence that includes a structured hierarchy

81
00:03:46.719 --> 00:03:50.080
of nested sequences and associated sequences,

82
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.689
all within a highly organized folder structure.

83
00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:56.409
While you'll most likely find that this tool doesn't work

84
00:03:56.409 --> 00:04:00.650
perfectly for your established pipeline or desired folder

85
00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.530
structure, I highly recommend you

86
00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.210
play with it at least once to observe

87
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.050
the way Unreal creates the parent sequence,

88
00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:13.409
nested child sequences, and the file and folder structures.
